  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1221 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nDry surface high pressure will continue to dominate the region\nthrough the remainder of the work week. High temperatures will\ncontinue to run around 10 degrees above normal, while low \ntemperatures trend warmer and will generally be 5-10 degrees \nabove normal. South winds will persist through the week, but\nshould be light enough to keep any potential fire weather \nconcerns in check.\n\nThe upper ridge will finally be shunted well to the east by this\nweekend, which will allow remnants from a tropical system off\nthe west coast of Mexico to eventually stream over the lower and\nmiddle Mississippi Valley Saturday into Sunday. Guidance\ncontinues to bounce around with the associated QPF placement and\namounts, but there will be a decent (~50%) chance of some rain \nthroughout the Quad State. The best chances continue to be late\nSaturday night into Sunday morning which for now bodes well for\nHalloween activities.\n\nAlthough we will have mid and high level moisture from the \ntropical remnant, the better surface moisture could get hung up \nsouth of the region which could limit rain chances and amounts\nfor the Quad State. Last night's ensemble data indicated that \nmuch of the region would see up to a half an inch of rainfall, \nbut the probabilities of even an inch were virtually nil. At this \ntime, there is little to no instability and the NBM has removed\nall mention of thunder from the forecast. The one concern is \nvery high precipitable water values (~200% of normal), which \ncould certainly support a heavier rainfall event. We will \ncontinue to monitor closely for any signs of more significant \nrainfall or thunder potential.\n\nTemperatures will remain mild over the weekend and into next\nMonday. Highs will be a bit lower with clouds and precipitation\npotential, but lows will be warmer with increased low-level \nmoisture and the cloud cover. \n\nThere are signs of another storm system to impact the region as\nearly as next Monday, but confidence in the details is quite \nlow that far out in the forecast. However, the NBM does have \n20-40% chances of showers lingering from Sunday night through \nMonday evening.",
